  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Planning a bathroom tile project? It’s helpful to know what kind of timeline you’re looking at so you can plan around your bathroom being out of commission. Generally, bathroom tile projects can take anywhere from several days to a couple of weeks, depending on the size and complexity. If you're just doing a floor, it'll be quicker than a full shower, floor, and wall revamp with intricate patterns. A big chunk of that time is actually for prep work and curing. First up is demolition and getting the substrate ready, which could mean repairing subfloors or installing new backer board. This is a crucial step that can't be rushed. Then comes waterproofing for wet areas like showers – that often needs specific cure times before tiling can even start. Once the tiles are set, the thinset adhesive needs to cure properly before grouting can happen, which is usually at least 24 hours. After grouting, that also needs time to cure before the area can be used or sealed. Skipping these cure times can lead to problems down the road! If your project involves moving plumbing or any structural changes, those elements might need permits and inspections, which can add time. We always aim to work efficiently, but also ensure every step is done correctly so your tile lasts. If you're curious about a specific timeline for your project, let's chat!"
    }
  ],
